{"kind":"stack","id":"s-repro","title":"Reproduction in plants and animals","theme":{"background":"#f7fbef","foreground":"#1f2d1a","font":"Verdana, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"Which part of a flower makes the pollen?"},{"type":"image","picto":"flower"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a1","label":"Stamen","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Category: a part of a flower. The stamen makes pollen, which is needed for reproduction."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a2","label":"Petal","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Pupils often think petals make pollen because they are the bright, colourful part."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a3","label":"Root","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Pupils may confuse the root with the reproductive parts because it is under the ground and holds the plant up."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Pollination"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Pollen moves from the stamen of one flower to the stigma of a flower, often carried by insects or wind."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What is the name of the cells that join together to start a new animal in sexual reproduction?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a1","label":"Egg and sperm","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Category: a pair of reproductive cells. An egg and a sperm join to form a new life."},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a2","label":"Blood and bone","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Pupils may think body tissues join to make a baby, not reproductive cells."},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a3","label":"Seed and leaf","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Seeds are a plant idea, so pupils may wrongly mix plant and animal reproduction."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"Which animal reproduces by laying eggs?"},{"type":"image","picto":"bird"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a1","label":"Hen","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Category: an animal that lays eggs. Hens lay eggs from which chicks hatch."},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a2","label":"Cow","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Pupils may think all farm animals lay eggs, but cows give birth to live young."},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a3","label":"Dog","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Pupils may think all four-legged animals are born the same way, but dogs give birth to live pups."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"A seed falls into the soil. Do you want to wait for rain to help it grow, or push up through the cracks at once?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a1","label":"Wait for rain","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a2","label":"Push up now","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You wait, and the rain soaks the soil. The seed swells and a tiny shoot appears."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"You push up through the dry soil, but it is hard to grow without water. The shoot stays small."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"How many young does a frog usually lay at one time?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a1","label":"Hundreds of eggs","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}],"why":"Category: a number of eggs. Frogs lay large numbers of eggs in water to improve survival."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a2","label":"Only one egg","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}],"why":"Pupils may think all animals have just one young, like humans or horses."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a3","label":"Exactly two eggs","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}],"why":"Pupils may guess a small number like two, not realising frogs lay far more."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-t","content":"Asexual reproduction"},{"type":"text","id":"c9-e","content":"A new living thing is made from one parent only, with no joining of cells. Plants such as potatoes can do this."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which plant can grow a new plant from a piece of its own stem or tuber?"},{"type":"image","picto":"tree"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a1","label":"Potato","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Category: a plant that spreads by tubers. A potato tuber can grow a new plant."},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a2","label":"Sunflower","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Pupils may think sunflowers spread the same way, but they grow from seeds only."},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a3","label":"Daisy","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Pupils may guess daisies because they are common, but they are mainly grown from seed."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"Well done, you finished the reproduction lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"You learned how plants and animals make new life, from pollination and eggs to growing from one parent. Keep exploring!"}],"actions":[]}}
